WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.109
A few versions of Batman's sidekick Robin
have appeared in live-action films, but there

00:00:05.109 --> 00:00:07.319
are a lot more we've still yet to see.

00:00:07.319 --> 00:00:10.040
Which one might best fit into the universe
of "The Batman"?

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:11.789
Keep watching for the answer.

00:00:11.789 --> 00:00:17.040
When director Matt Reeves' "The Batman" premieres
in the U.S. on March 4, 2022, it will mark

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:21.320
the first standalone live-action Batman movie
since Christopher Nolan's "The Dark Knight

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.180
Rises" in 2012.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:27.610
Robert Pattinson will be donning the cowl,
he'll be teaming up with Zoe Kravitz as Catwoman,

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:29.480
and they'll be taking on a whole host of threats.

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:34.079
There's Paul Dano's The Riddler, a Zodiac
killer-inspired take on the character who's

00:00:34.079 --> 00:00:36.199
trying to expose Gotham's elites.

00:00:36.199 --> 00:00:40.629
There's also Colin Farrell as The Penguin,
and John Turturro as the gangster Carmine

00:00:40.629 --> 00:00:41.899
Falcone.

00:00:41.899 --> 00:00:46.239
Batman will luckily get a little moral support
from Bruce Wayne's longtime butler, Alfred

00:00:46.239 --> 00:00:48.329
Pennyworth, played by Andy Serkis.

00:00:48.329 --> 00:00:52.399
As is always the case with a new Batman movie,
one big question is whether Robin will be

00:00:52.399 --> 00:00:53.780
part of the story.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:58.699
Unfortunately for fans of Batman's stalwart
sidekick, Robin isn't in the mix this time,

00:00:58.699 --> 00:01:00.550
though Reeves hasn't ruled him out in the
future.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:01.769
"Holy heartbreak."

00:01:01.769 --> 00:01:05.479
So far, Robin has shown up just a few times
in live-action movies.

00:01:05.479 --> 00:01:09.880
He was played by Douglas Croft and Johnny
Duncan in the often-forgotten "Batman" movie

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:16.130
serials in the 1940s, and Burt Ward played
Robin in the 1966 live-action "Batman" film

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:17.799
alongside Adam West.

00:01:17.799 --> 00:01:24.060
Chris O'Donnell played him in both 1995's
"Batman Forever" and 1997's "Batman &amp; Robin."

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:28.260
Like Croft and Ward's Robins, this was the
Dick Grayson version of the character, a traveling

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.810
acrobat whose family is tragically killed.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:35.560
"The Dark Knight Rises" had an original character
with similarities to Robin, John Blake, who

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.759
was played by Joseph Gordon-Levitt.

00:01:37.759 --> 00:01:40.840
He's a Gotham City cop who works with Batman
and later reveals…

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:42.500
"You should use your full name.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:44.170
I like that name.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:45.170
Robin."

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:46.170
"Thanks."

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:50.260
That leaves a slew of DC Comics versions of
Robin that may have shown up on TV and in

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:54.020
animated productions, but have never appeared
in a live-action film.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:57.229
Pattinson told Collider he's up for adding
Robin, with conditions:

00:01:57.229 --> 00:01:59.479
"Yeah, but he has to be 13.

00:01:59.479 --> 00:02:01.159
That's the only way I'll accept it.

00:02:01.159 --> 00:02:04.939
No, I love 'Death in the Family' and stuff,
but I think it'd be so cool.

00:02:04.939 --> 00:02:07.770
Also people are so scared of it, but it's
kind of exciting.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:09.629
I think it would be a really fun addition."

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:14.421
"Death in the Family" is the 1988 comics story
in which the Jason Todd version of Robin is

00:02:14.421 --> 00:02:15.780
killed by the Joker.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:20.230
Todd is an orphan and the second individual
to take up the Robin mantle after Dick Grayson

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:21.290
aged out of it.

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:26.041
Later, Todd is resurrected as the vigilante
superhero Red Hood who, unlike Batman, has

00:02:26.041 --> 00:02:28.280
no moral qualms about killing criminals.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:32.540
The Jason Todd arc, including the resurrection,
would seem to fit right in with the dark tone

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.720
Reeves is establishing with "The Batman."

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.159
There's also the third Robin, Tim Drake.

00:02:37.159 --> 00:02:41.379
He's the third iteration of Robin, who after
figuring out both Batman and Robin's real

00:02:41.379 --> 00:02:45.650
identities, approaches Bruce Wayne and asks
to become his next sidekick.

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:49.620
Bruce trains Drake into one of the best martial
artists and detectives in the world.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:54.610
Drake eventually ages out of the role, too,
after which he takes on a new name, Red Robin.

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:58.689
If Reeves really wanted to throw a curveball,
he'd go with the Stephanie Brown version of

00:02:58.689 --> 00:02:59.689
Robin.

00:02:59.689 --> 00:03:03.400
She's a street vigilante and the daughter
of the supervillain Cluemaster, who starts

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.920
her career as the costumed hero Spoiler.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.830
She briefly claims the Robin mantle but is
fired for disobeying orders.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:11.659
She later becomes Batgirl.

00:03:11.659 --> 00:03:15.739
Or there's the fifth and most recent Robin,
Damian Wayne, Bruce Wayne's son with Talia

00:03:15.739 --> 00:03:16.840
al-Ghul.

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:21.239
Since Talia appeared in "The Dark Knight Rises"
and had a brief relationship with Bruce, and

00:03:21.239 --> 00:03:26.060
since "The Batman" is a wholesale reboot of
the franchise, this one seems less likely.

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:29.750
There's also Carrie Kelley from Frank Miller's
"The Dark Knight Returns," who spent some

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:33.129
time as Robin in a version of Batman's very
late career.

00:03:33.129 --> 00:03:37.459
It's also entirely possible that Reeves could
follow Nolan and go with something new.

00:03:37.459 --> 00:03:41.220
Before he can do that, "The Batman" must first
deliver at the box office.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:44.810
The good news is the critics mostly love it
so far.

